<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Uteve</h1><p class="st-meta">1490 CE – 1890 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Shona kingdom behind the port of Sofala whose rulers, the Sachiteve, charged the Portuguese for passage to the gold fairs of the interior.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Uteve emerged in the late 15th century when a branch of the ruling house in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> lands broke away and established itself between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwe plateau</a> and the coast. Its position astride the routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> to the inland goldfields made it a key intermediary for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> and then Portuguese traders.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king, called the Sachiteve (Quiteve to the Portuguese), was a sacred ruler bound by strict ritual; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joao-dos-santos">Joao dos Santos</a>, who lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> in the 1590s, described royal ceremonies and the custom by which a king who became infirm or blemished was expected to take poison. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spirit-medium">Spirit mediums</a> and ancestor cults were central. The Portuguese paid regular tribute, the curva, for the right to trade through the kingdom.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Uteve was weakened by succession disputes and in the 19th century fell under the domination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaza-empire">Gaza Nguni</a>. By around 1890 its lands were included in the territory of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mozambique-company">Mozambique Company</a> within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1490 CE – 1890 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.35%;width:7.96%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1490 CE – 1890 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Shona religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shona (Ndau)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Quiteve, Teve</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1690&amp;focus=kingdom-of-uteve" aria-label="Kingdom of Uteve on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5722 2704 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Uteve lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5833 3185h0M5880 3217h0M5861 3386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6042 2960h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6042 2960h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mozambique. <a href="/atlas?year=1690&amp;focus=kingdom-of-uteve">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
